# Never pass `preset:COLOR` strings to `<Image source={{ uri }} />`

## The Bug

`profile-pic.tsx` lets users pick a color swatch instead of a photo. It saves
`avatar_url` in the format `"preset:#2196F3"`. This is intentional — it means
"render a solid color circle as the avatar."

**However**, passing this string directly to React Native's `<Image>` crashes
the native iOS image loader:

```
No suitable URL request handler found for preset:%232196F3
```

`%23` is URL-encoded `#`, so `preset:#2196F3` hits the RCT bridge, which has
no handler for the `preset:` scheme.

## The Fix — always use `parsePresetColor` before rendering an avatar

```tsx
// ✅ CORRECT — detect preset: before touching <Image>
import { parsePresetColor } from '../../components/shared/Avatar';

const presetColor = parsePresetColor(avatarUrl); // returns "#2196F3" or null

if (presetColor) {
  return <View style={{ backgroundColor: presetColor, borderRadius: size / 2 }} />;
}
if (avatarUrl) {
  return <Image source={{ uri: avatarUrl }} />;   // safe — not a preset: URL
}

// ❌ WRONG — passes "preset:#2196F3" to <Image>, crashes on iOS
return <Image source={{ uri: avatarUrl }} />;
```

## `parsePresetColor` lives in `components/shared/Avatar.tsx`

```ts
export function parsePresetColor(uri: string | null | undefined): string | null {
  if (!uri) return null;
  return uri.startsWith('preset:') ? uri.slice('preset:'.length) : null;
}
```

## Every avatar-rendering site must call this first

- `components/shared/Avatar.tsx` — ✅ already handled
- `components/shared/AvatarStack.tsx` — ✅ already handled
- `app/(tabs)/index.tsx` `HeaderAvatar` — ✅ already handled
- Any **new** screen or component that renders a user avatar must follow the
  same pattern. Never do `<Image source={{ uri: profile.avatar_url }} />`
  without first calling `parsePresetColor`.
